        ":question/problem": "What is the value of $x$ if $e^{x - 3}=2^5?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-3} & ={2}^{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{x-3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({2}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\ln \\left( e^a \\right) = a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx-3 = \\ln \\left( 2^5 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ and rewrite the right-hand side using the power law of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({2}^{5}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({2}^{5})+3 \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5\\ln (2) + 3.$"
