          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4-5y} & =21 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{4-5y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(21)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n4-5y=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(21).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-5y & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(21) \\\\ -5y & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(21)-4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(21)-4) \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(21)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(21).$"
        }
      ],
